    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "PrEP (or its commercial name, Truvada), a daily pill that can reduce risk of HIV infection by up to 92% took the HIV research field by storm after its approval by the FDA in 2012. However, four years after its approval for pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP, the process of preventing HIV before exposure to the virus), PrEP use within the MSM (men who have sex with men) community in the U.S. remains low while HIV incidence rates remain stagnant. By placing a greater emphasis of LGBT cultural competency within the medical field and bridging the gap between the MSM and medical communities rooted in the 1980s AIDS epidemic, American medicine has an opportunity to not only virtually stop a virus that has eluded it for far too long, but also better serve a community that it has largely overlooked.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
